#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189104} :activity/id #uuid "cd68d011-8836-5efd-ab3b-ba09c700a3e9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049739 :topic/id #uuid "0fe4fee1-477c-5881-86c7-1921fd5fff4e" :topic/math-academy-id 3860} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188203 :question/id #uuid "012abf23-a2df-5479-a38f-3c76c099a39f" :question/math-academy-id "e-524"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d868c89-ca9e-589d-917e-cfec472a44f6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050411}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542182}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "da66f4c9-69eb-512a-b43d-9289b5f8fb5a" :step/math-academy-id 1853 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189375 :tutorial/content "When constructing a differential equation to model a particular phenomenon, we sometimes combine several pieces of information to create the correct model.\n\nConsider the following situation:\n\n*The common cold virus spreads through a particular country. The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t$ (in days) since the outbreak started. \nAdditionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**.*\n\nWe want to find a differential equation that models $P(t),$ the number of infected people. We note the following:\n\n- The statement describes the *rate* at which the *number of infected people* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t.$ Therefore, if $I(t)$ represents the rate at which infections increase, then\n\n$$\nI(t) = kt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant.\n\n- Additionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**. Therefore, if $D(t)$ represents the rate at which infections decrease, then\n\n$$\nD(t) = 100.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that describes this phenomenon is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & ={{k{t}^{2}-100}_{⏟}}_{I(t)-D(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough we introduced the functions $I(t)$ and $D(t)$ here, we can usually write down the appropriate differential equation straight away. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a7ad3b5-e4de-56c0-8fc9-8a9b2c976094" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6270 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "37206e3d-9800-567e-82f6-eed74a977f1f" :step/math-academy-id 38001 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189105}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188290 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187994 :question/id #uuid "67d01a20-4e4b-5b03-a2a3-f4b21cd81662" :question/math-academy-id "e-10072"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9609247-0e03-57ea-b5ae-e4d8317f21cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501160}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Contextual Problems Into Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "9da430dc-8eca-5286-8278-c764eba32b5d" :step/math-academy-id 38002 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189245}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188291 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187995 :question/id #uuid "1b0483b6-a42d-51fb-9ccd-fe6cb2a7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10075"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba211dba-23ad-5755-b1e3-082e13be59a2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048542}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542180}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Three-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "53525e67-0e77-588f-88a9-1bda7f5964de" :step/math-academy-id 38012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188292 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187996 :question/id #uuid "b657adbb-c3c4-54c2-8c2c-44ccc0bf9c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10076"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474f9c82-4ae7-58e0-9457-6bda9cab8973" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050975}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542145}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Coordinate Plane Problems Into Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "ccce909c-08ed-53ce-a30b-e357e8de3d59" :step/math-academy-id 38016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189439 :tutorial/content "Let's recall some basic facts from geometry in the $xy$-plane:\n\n- The distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the origin $(0,0)$ equals $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}.$\n\n- The (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $x$-axis equals $|y|.$\n\n- Similarly, the (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $y$-axis equals $|x|.$\n\n- The slope of the line that passes through $(x,y)$ and the origin equals $\\dfrac{y}{x}.$\n\nWe sometimes need to use these basic facts when constructing differential equations described in a geometric context. \n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "b03d6daa-af90-5f14-bf0f-929da8526673" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8152 :tutorial/title "Recalling Some Facts From Elementary Geometry"} :step/id #uuid "d499c1d3-7173-5509-b128-c42561cce342" :step/math-academy-id 53348 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189108}}] :activity/title "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188455}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536021} :activity/id #uuid "f0450c29-614d-584c-bd9d-e5e05615fc5b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049739 :topic/id #uuid "0fe4fee1-477c-5881-86c7-1921fd5fff4e" :topic/math-academy-id 3860} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee8b8c0d-1ee4-5b74-98ae-a39f339006ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373836} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fce73c-e33e-526e-ae9d-d604ddfe425e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-Ks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373837} {:answer/id #uuid "de1fd09f-93d4-5865-b24c-0e86883ac345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373838} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5e3a4a-13c7-547b-b482-926b18785c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373839} {:answer/id #uuid "344b3dd6-8a8c-5b38-83e6-babf864485c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373840}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373839} :answer-field/id #uuid "722e2706-48b9-573e-a5f7-472963351881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0936be88-f149-5a42-9d58-500c046543ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-143135" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate inversely proportional to its side length. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **decreases** at a rate **inversely proportional** to **its side length**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate *inversely proportional its side length*. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{s}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{2{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2}>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "17e24c4d-fbe4-5267-b369-1dc13301b94c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00359f0d-5c3b-50c1-a12e-737bc41221da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535436} {:answer/id #uuid "3c96294f-e5cd-511a-981b-9f5670bd9a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=k{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535527} {:answer/id #uuid "7e897ffd-840e-5b35-92d6-f6d2bd060f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535635} {:answer/id #uuid "ec89e486-9020-5137-9ccb-884bab47d61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=k{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535800} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ffbb76-ca6f-595c-aa26-024793132497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=kxy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535635} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce498ff-bed5-5a31-b8d4-a763ddd397a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5deb4667-ba88-546b-92cb-11999248cbcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-38329" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the distance from $P$ to the origin. What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *distance from $P(x,y)$ to the origin* is given by $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "52e7e185-8a07-5891-8e9c-c1bf0de98ed5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca4cfd4-07ce-53b0-ac05-7ef895c2e528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535582} {:answer/id #uuid "85d4c3b1-bd0d-5ede-ab88-6b4e89d39b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535648} {:answer/id #uuid "c0dfbcc4-c509-5a61-956d-8bd3f7248123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535729} {:answer/id #uuid "c85ccc6a-9e93-5f3b-a97f-d6c05f64a53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535736} {:answer/id #uuid "de7decf8-37f7-5b67-adfa-4152ced74e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=Ks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535582} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddc65ecf-2fc0-57a8-93c3-d6c4c4e692c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d65b7da-354a-5fe6-8886-5852de4be621" :question/math-academy-id "q-80488" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to its perimeter. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **increases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to its **perimeter**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to its *perimeter.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{(\\frac{k}{4s})}{2s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{8{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{8}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "677b36df-44d3-546b-99e0-9f2b342dd41c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eace44a-9542-5986-9bb4-4a121809282b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535551} {:answer/id #uuid "5184e724-e665-5cf4-9d18-27d961cdc95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535561} {:answer/id #uuid "537b4f55-d454-56dc-93a5-b86d06a5d655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535565} {:answer/id #uuid "71ee97c0-dcb3-5432-b6ad-3f697f647db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535609} {:answer/id #uuid "a39bc14a-c886-535c-9199-d7ab15d57b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535551} :answer-field/id #uuid "13b9af92-d428-5ecd-80c2-21192da8e8da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "907d5b64-98c1-552d-a43d-a699db06e688" :question/math-academy-id "q-119167" :question/problem "The volume of a sphere decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $k>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*Its **volume decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\pi k{r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b67684ce-1284-5a67-8a4d-2617a5d04b2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49a327b9-75a5-53f6-8b43-cf618bcf3524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373801} {:answer/id #uuid "1df99bf7-929a-5903-abcf-591bb526f29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373802} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbf909f-462f-5518-8013-a606f7cf41aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373803} {:answer/id #uuid "3d49d7ac-08ee-59ab-9b44-461517ebb9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373804} {:answer/id #uuid "708d1932-73c6-5f7d-ae3b-5e1d6d9045b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373803} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ed6ed5-0a52-5e29-8c5e-f028a3e0baf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "deb34ff2-c563-5932-95e2-cff733776593" :question/math-academy-id "q-143125" :question/problem "The volume of a cube increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the length of its edge. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the edge length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cube **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **length of its edge**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cube* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional to the length of its edge.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube is given by\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ 3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{3{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{3}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ee2e114c-cdf4-53db-b71c-1fd113ce8364" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536010}}] :activity/title "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535433}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542271} :activity/id #uuid "f0e0b381-c4f7-544d-803f-1f89f6490699" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049739 :topic/id #uuid "0fe4fee1-477c-5881-86c7-1921fd5fff4e" :topic/math-academy-id 3860} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2010fc61-4364-5862-a96d-774075019bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541487} {:answer/id #uuid "705dbc1d-a754-523f-92e0-8940c3b04884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541650} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1b73b6-ad80-5c74-ab55-18bad8b8f274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541858} {:answer/id #uuid "d69ff15d-ef03-5444-8c1a-9ce4ef60002c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541881} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f93573-3c61-5ff8-b156-76483ae8624a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541487} :answer-field/id #uuid "e60fe765-3ba7-5b2e-a3cc-67f6b659f5d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8d62c5b-3e1c-59af-b194-160b1241f14c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143132" :question/problem "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to the cube of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the cube of its **radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the cube of its *radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{3} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{3}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1a243f40-c3c6-58d7-bac3-52d3bb082cf4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542255}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f1b9c6-6af0-5a2a-af00-95a3f317bc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{40}{|y|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541592} {:answer/id #uuid "6004d047-d373-53e3-95ab-5c21565957f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{10}{|x|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541617} {:answer/id #uuid "69b6b7e0-47f8-5d3f-90c7-9cc539874ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{5}{|x|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541634} {:answer/id #uuid "98ba0312-7934-5cd5-a8d0-964450104886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{5|y|}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541739} {:answer/id #uuid "a9291194-9f4c-5ec3-89de-d6aac381c602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{|x|}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541772}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541617} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa9844e-bf08-5151-a0ef-3ca82c6b8c21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "500cc9b6-df77-5d16-8dd2-a74988fb81dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-143140" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. The derivative at the point $(2,8)$ equals $5.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis* is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional* to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot \\frac{1}{|x|}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k}{|x|}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=5$ when at the point $(2,8).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5=\\frac{k}{|2|}\\,⟹\\,k=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{10}{|x|}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2b8f298c-89ca-5efd-8419-026734b35b16"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542255 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c4829e-9ff5-5c77-8160-757948c34286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=3|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373864} {:answer/id #uuid "7e16602c-ea2a-5cbc-a3ed-6fcf2ad9b012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=8|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373865} {:answer/id #uuid "80b53aba-6c00-50c6-bef0-87cc4e4f247b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373866} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0ceb4b-3c4e-561d-b09c-109e90eae4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{24|y|}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373867} {:answer/id #uuid "a9af8465-e86d-58d5-908e-3721348d588c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373865} :answer-field/id #uuid "81b73e2e-d50c-5e49-8505-3ee73aad8826" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e6b89f7e-afce-55f3-8c93-dbf9b7119d01" :question/math-academy-id "q-143139" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. The derivative at the point $(3,-5)$ equals $24.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis* is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot |x|\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k|x|.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=24$ when at the point $(3,-5).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24=k|3|\\,⟹\\,k=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=8|x|.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "52ea8b3b-19b7-5968-bcaa-348c34bcb384" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542243}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031ea96a-3096-54b0-ad2a-4f96e9d8183b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=k-h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541431} {:answer/id #uuid "25636b1a-6d91-597e-9b19-e787394d985e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-kh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541499} {:answer/id #uuid "9415b7a9-5a1a-57a3-803c-ddf72eef3856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541729} {:answer/id #uuid "bab4b395-1c32-5273-a354-4159ddc0b2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541808} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7edd2e-8ea0-5615-81cd-8f820d79ec79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-k{h}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541892}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541499} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef6a3fe-d635-518d-8afa-e08f3e08c478" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a29a3c61-aa9f-590b-908a-6227844310f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-119170" :question/problem "The volume of a cylinder decreases at a rate proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the height, $h(t),$ in this process. \nAssume that the radius of the cylinder does not change and that $k>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **decreases** at a rate **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate *proportional to the volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and using the fact that the radius of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ \\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-k(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k\\pi {r}^{2}h}{\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-kh.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "53b991cc-3a22-5116-adab-cd17f3c705cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1973d584-80ce-553d-a306-6d2016596bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541473} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb5aa8c-19fb-505d-99d0-6d1e5e391d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541585} {:answer/id #uuid "86e0e1ed-497b-5bc3-9be6-1e1fb1057fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541694} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cbfbe5-1faa-5f71-b7e8-8941f3018693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541770} {:answer/id #uuid "feafdf56-0689-5b42-9564-9aa9c5d0dfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541585} :answer-field/id #uuid "60e99a5a-7fef-5572-9023-63b84b45c7f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3da31f55-8d6c-5a19-aa68-60451055a7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-119166" :question/problem "The volume of a cylinder decreases at a rate that's proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that the height of the cylinder does not change and that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **decreases** at a rate that's **proportional** to the **square of its radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to the square of its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and the fact that the height of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi hr\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\pi hr\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi hr} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{2\\pi h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi h}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "88e7fdbd-88d3-5a0c-b233-89990b64230f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542256}}] :activity/title "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541426}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547103} :activity/id #uuid "d6022a2b-4bf1-5e53-8971-24eeeee3f3d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049739 :topic/id #uuid "0fe4fee1-477c-5881-86c7-1921fd5fff4e" :topic/math-academy-id 3860} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2010fc61-4364-5862-a96d-774075019bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541487} {:answer/id #uuid "705dbc1d-a754-523f-92e0-8940c3b04884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541650} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1b73b6-ad80-5c74-ab55-18bad8b8f274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541858} {:answer/id #uuid "d69ff15d-ef03-5444-8c1a-9ce4ef60002c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541881} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f93573-3c61-5ff8-b156-76483ae8624a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541487} :answer-field/id #uuid "e60fe765-3ba7-5b2e-a3cc-67f6b659f5d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8d62c5b-3e1c-59af-b194-160b1241f14c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143132" :question/problem "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to the cube of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the cube of its **radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the cube of its *radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{3} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{3}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "08819b5d-c31d-5f8f-a25c-647533ce685e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "566b270e-601c-556c-909e-228f098bd523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500642} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb54fc0-7b29-5b8d-9591-693a024ce49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500902} {:answer/id #uuid "d478dfbd-05e0-5dc2-bc83-3caf65a81571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=kxy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500919} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c89197-063d-5efb-987f-b5a6be72e4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500962} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd59d23-9e44-5a2b-9d57-caf1ff79fe38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501013} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cf447a-a943-5b06-b1a6-df34fd52eceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7fc67f3e-fcb8-5740-a171-eb4e59b85b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-143110" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis* is given by ${y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot {y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2caacd20-fec4-513f-bebd-77f9f2aecd7e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547172}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa2b59f-32da-5f61-9e8c-c999179869a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373829} {:answer/id #uuid "77cab6e1-05d0-59ce-bfed-0248ceddcab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373830} {:answer/id #uuid "95aa500e-03cb-500e-ab89-a98ef4fc2170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373831} {:answer/id #uuid "34c6a305-ea32-55e9-8059-937e6d3212f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373832} {:answer/id #uuid "ffce36b0-40f6-5025-80cc-075308f5e7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373831} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa7450e-ccaf-5284-b104-8cbf050240f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2bf1eec-2fea-5deb-b983-28820449be19" :question/math-academy-id "q-143133" :question/problem "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to its circumference. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to its **circumference**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to its *circumference.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{2\\pi r}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{2\\pi r} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{4{\\pi }^{2}{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{4{\\pi }^{2}}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cbacfd83-8629-5916-ac5d-e566d37cbba0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547175}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547172 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93daca08-5346-5c8d-9d08-4c8a920607fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373815} {:answer/id #uuid "2a63d060-2d42-5042-b169-688f85c60c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373816} {:answer/id #uuid "4a592f41-9583-5de8-a838-256ec9464b73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373817} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab4dfc8-bdfb-5f3c-bdaf-6e28e990adca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373818} {:answer/id #uuid "c7baf46c-24c9-551d-8c0b-fa86bb82933a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373816} :answer-field/id #uuid "7631cd09-e0b1-5b28-8edc-c8fe29726e41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ebd4947c-3eb3-5ec1-a356-73f943c86767" :question/math-academy-id "q-143128" :question/problem "The volume of a cylinder increases at a rate proportional to the radius of its base. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that the height of the cylinder does not change and that $k>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **radius of its base**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the radius of its base.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kr,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and using the fact that the height of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi rh\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =kr \\\\ 2\\pi rh\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =kr \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{kr}{2\\pi rh} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{2\\pi h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi h}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f0052809-97ca-51d0-969f-77faff2a4d64" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547175 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bc77990-b298-505f-b5b6-6eb164aa042c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=15({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373857} {:answer/id #uuid "45be93aa-c0b6-57c6-b243-903709eba10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=5\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373858} {:answer/id #uuid "a288bd4c-2041-5dc4-9891-077a8a8c988f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373859} {:answer/id #uuid "23b3abcf-2ffe-5f04-abca-39f486a5cac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373860} {:answer/id #uuid "e40cd54b-a715-5581-a060-b2d06897778b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373861} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f7efba-0461-52b6-9d0a-d0990d07f9c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b00489a-807a-5221-8dab-d57f2fcb225b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143138" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the distance from $P$ to the origin. The derivative at the point $(-3,4)$ equals $15.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *distance from $P$ to the origin* is given by $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=15$ when at the point $(-3,4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =k\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\ 15 & =k\\cdot 5 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fa547646-60d3-574c-951d-eeb33327ad0d"}] :activity/title "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546249}]}
